  - [1mExisting call sites + global error capture[0m — adds [38;5;153mwindow.onerror[39m and [38;5;153munhandledrejection[39m handlers routed through the
    logger. Buys you the errors you most need in production, which are exactly the ones that never pass through a call
    site you wrote. Costs two handlers.
  - [1mExisting call sites only[0m — a straight swap of the four [38;5;153mconsole.*[39m calls. Cheapest, but an uncaught [38;5;153mTypeError[39m in the
    submit handler still vanishes silently.
  - [1mThe above plus lifecycle and timing[0m — page load, submit duration, a per-page-load session ID stamped on every record
    so you can correlate one user's sequence. Buys real debuggability; costs a handful of extra call sites in a 28-line
    file.

  Cheap to change later: adding call sites, adding the session ID. Nothing here is expensive to reverse — this is the
  least locked-in decision of the four.

  [1mMy recommendation: existing call sites + global error capture + a session ID on every record.[0m The global handlers are
  the actual answer to "debug production issues," and the session ID costs about three lines while making a console dump
  from a user readable as a sequence rather than loose lines. I'd skip the timing instrumentation — there's nothing
  slow here yet to measure.
